
## CHALLENGE 1: GETTING CLEAN DATA
###########################################
I applied downsampling, where the simulator records way too many frames of the car going straight without actually turning
so we defined a threshold, any steering value below 0.02 was considered going straight and we filtered that out by 70%.

On first attempt, the data recorded had a huge spike at 0.0 steering, meaning most of what was recorded was a straight line movement

this imbalance is the reason why most self driving models fail on first training run

the model learns to "always go straight" because of the loss function is MSE, it minimizes error by just
predicting 0.0 steering for everything. Mathematically, it worked but failed in practice

To fix this imbalance, we can do a couple of things:
    1. collect better data 
        a. slow laps, stay in center, more turns
        b. 2-3 laps in reverse
    2. Downsample straight driving rows, delete rows with 0.0 steering

in my first attempt, i had 6706 straight frames with almost no turning frames, so decided on deleting that
data and start over with better driving as mentioned above


Even after multiple test runs and cleaner laps, 0.00 was still by far the most dominating factor,
So to fix this I implemented a threshold of 0.02, anything less than this was considered straight 
movement.
The other threshold defined was to keep only 20% of the original straight line movement


